<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The simulation, and it has NO reference to Godot.

    That absence is the architecture. `crates/sim` had zero engine dependencies
    as a rule rather than an accident, and under GDScript the only way to keep
    that was a source scan that read the files and grepped for `Vector2`. Here
    it is a property of the build: this project does not reference GodotSharp,
    so a line that reaches for the engine does not compile. An invalid state
    made unrepresentable rather than detected.

    It also means the whole simulation is testable, and the trajectory runner
    runnable, without the engine in the loop at all.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>

    <!-- A warning that is not an error is a warning nobody reads. -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest-recommended</AnalysisLevel>

    <!--
      Determinism, stated to the compiler.

      The simulation must reproduce bit-for-bit, so the JIT must not be allowed
      to contract a multiply and an add into an FMA or otherwise reassociate
      floats. .NET makes no such transformations on `double`/`float` arithmetic
      by default — unlike a C compiler with fast-math — and there is no switch
      here that turns them on. This block exists to say that was checked rather
      than assumed, and `Determinism` in the test suite is what holds it.
    -->
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The suite drives state that only the simulation's own systems are meant to
      write — a crew's site, a visitor's hotel, how far a road has got. Those
      setters are `internal` to say exactly that: a caller reads them and a
      system writes them.

      Opening them to the test assembly rather than making them public keeps the
      distinction real. A test that sets up "this gang is stranded" is doing what
      a system would have done to reach that state, which is the only honest way
      to test the rule that fires when it happens.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="RedRepublic.Sim.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
